## A link: an address and some words

The link is what turned a pile of documents into a web. It’s an `<a>` element (anchor) with an `href` attribute (hypertext reference). Two things matter about it: where it goes and what is written inside it.

**Link text** should make sense on its own. People who use a screen reader often ask it for a list of all the links on a page, and if that list says “click here” and “read more” twenty times over, it’s no use. Write “More about boundary surveys”, “Download prices (PDF, 120 KB)”, “Book a consultation”.

**The address** comes in several kinds. Try the diagram: change the page’s address and the `href` value, and see where the link goes.

<LinkResolver />

The kinds in brief:

- **absolute** is a full address with `https://`: it leads to one particular page wherever you start from. It suits other websites;
- **relative** is `contact.html` or `../contact.html`: it’s worked out from the address of the current page. It suits links inside your own site, because nothing breaks if you move to another domain;
- **from the site root** is `/contact.html`: it’s worked out from the home page, not from the current page. It’s handy when pages sit in different folders;
- **an anchor** is `#contacts`: a place on this same page;
- **special schemes**: `mailto:kate@riverbend.example` opens an email, and `tel:+440000000000` dials a number.

If a link must open in a new tab, you write `target="_blank"`. Do that only when it’s really needed (to keep someone from losing a form they’re filling in, say), and warn people: an unexpected new window gets in the way of someone using a screen reader. It used to be usual to add `rel="noopener"` to such a link. Modern browsers do that themselves, but the habit does no harm.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why is a relative link better than an absolute one on your own site?</summary>

It isn’t tied to the site’s address, so nothing breaks if you move to another domain or another folder.

</details>

## Anchors: a link to a place on the page

A link doesn’t have to lead to another page. It can lead to a spot on this one. Give the element you want to reach a unique `id`, and write the link with a hash sign: `href="#contacts"`.

```html
<a href="#contacts">Get in touch</a>
...
<aside id="contacts">...</aside>
```

The browser scrolls the page to the element with that `id`. The rules: an `id` is unique on the page, has no spaces, and is best written in Latin letters. If a link with a hash doesn’t work, the cause is almost always a typo: the `id` and the `href` don’t match.

## Images

An image goes in with the single element `<img>`:

```html
<img src="foto.jpg" width="160" height="160" alt="Kate measuring a plot boundary">
```

The right file format is half the job.

- **JPEG** is for photographs: it compresses well but has no transparency;
- **PNG** is for graphics and screenshots, and it has transparency;
- **SVG** is for icons and diagrams: a vector image that stays sharp on any screen and weighs a few kilobytes;
- **WebP and AVIF** are modern formats that compress better than JPEG at the same quality. All modern browsers support them;
- **GIF** is outdated: for animation today you use video, which weighs about a tenth as much.

### alt: words in place of the image

The `alt` attribute is the image put into words. A screen reader reads it, and the browser shows it if the image fails to load (you’ll see that in the practice, because the files don’t exist). The rules are simple:

- if the image **carries meaning**, describe what is in it and why it’s there: “Kate measuring a plot boundary”, “Map of the route: 200 yards from the bus stop, then turn left”;
- if it’s **decorative** (a flourish, a background), write an empty `alt=""`. Don’t remove the attribute: without it a screen reader reads out the file name, something like “IMG_2043.jpg”;
- if the image **works as a link or a button**, describe the action, not the picture: “Call”, not “telephone receiver”;
- don’t begin with “image of” or “photo of”, because the screen reader already says it’s an image;
- don’t stuff it with keywords. That hurts both people and search.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what do you write in the alt of a decorative image?</summary>

An empty value: the attribute is there, but has nothing in it. It has to be there, or the screen reader will read out the file name.

</details>

### Sizes and lazy loading

Until an image has loaded, the browser doesn’t know how much room to leave for it. Remember the “jumping layout” from the last lesson of the internet part? You cure it by writing `width` and `height` in pixels yourself. From them the browser works out the proportions in advance and reserves the space. CSS can adjust the real size later.

Images far down the page can wait until the person scrolls near them: `loading="lazy"`. An image on the first screen, by contrast, must never be lazy, or it would hold up the first paint.

### srcset and picture: the right size for every screen

If you send a phone an image 2000 pixels wide, you waste its data. So you prepare one image in several sizes and list them in `srcset`:

```html
<img src="foto-800.jpg"
     srcset="foto-480.jpg 480w, foto-800.jpg 800w, foto-1200.jpg 1200w"
     sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 40rem, 100vw"
     width="800" height="600" alt="…">
```

Here `480w` is the file’s width in pixels, and `sizes` hints at how much room the image will take on the page. The browser knows the window’s width and the screen’s density, so it picks a file itself, as in the diagram above. When you need a different format, or a different crop for different screens, you wrap the image in `<picture>` with several `<source>` elements, and the fallback `<img>` stays inside.

A caption for an image is made with the pair `<figure>` and `<figcaption>`: the figure ties the image to its caption.

## Video and audio

The `<video>` and `<audio>` elements are built into the browser:

```html
<video controls width="640" poster="cover.jpg">
  <source src="clip.webm" type="video/webm">
  <source src="clip.mp4" type="video/mp4">
  <track kind="captions" src="clip.vtt" srclang="en" label="English">
  Your browser can’t play this video: <a href="clip.mp4">download it</a>.
</video>
```

What’s in there:

- `controls` shows the play buttons;
- `poster` is an image shown before the video starts;
- several `<source>` elements give the browser a choice of format;
- `<track>` holds captions. They’re needed by people who are deaf or hard of hearing, and by everyone who watches without sound (on a train, at work);
- the text inside the element is seen by anyone whose browser can’t play video.

Don’t turn on autoplay with sound: it annoys people and gets in the way of anyone using a screen reader. A video that starts by itself must be silent, and must be possible to stop.

Often a video isn’t kept on your own site but embedded from a video host with an `<iframe>`. That’s convenient, but know the price: your page now talks to someone else’s server, which may collect data about your visitors. That is why the videos in this course open only after a click.

## Practice

Back to your 2005 page. It came out well structured but connected to nothing. Connect it: the menu items should lead to their sections, the main action button to the contacts, and the footer should let people go back to the top and open the privacy notice. Then add a photo and the credentials from the brief.

There are no images in our preview, because it doesn’t go to the network. So in place of each image you’ll see its description, which is a good chance to check that it makes sense on its own. On a real site the files sit next to the page and load. In the project archive you can download, you’ll put your own photos next to it under the same names.

We ask you to connect “at least three” sections, but you can connect them all, which is better for your visitor.

## What was wrong with 2005

Images with no `alt` and no sizes were normal. The page jumped while loading, and for blind users it turned into a string of file names. Links that said “here” and “more” were normal too. None of this was a technical limit. It was habit: when you take it that an image means only what the eye sees, you don’t think about anyone else. Today automated tools check for these habits.
